alex_chen: heads-up - I'm seeing sporadic JSON.parse errors in the browser client when streaming from /v1/stream. Only happens when token refresh coincides with a chunk boundary.

maria_g: ugh. repro steps?

alex_chen: steps: 1) Use the browser SSE polyfill (EventSource fallback). 2) Start a long completion. 3) Force token refresh by expiring local token (simulate short TTL). 4) mid-stream we get a chunk like `}\n\nsamir: wait, so token refresh injects something into the stream?

alex_chen: not injecting, but the auth middleware triggers a reconnect. The server responds with a 401, client reconnects, and in-between the previous stream ends with a partial JSON fragment. Our simple "line-delimited object per event" parser tries JSON.parse on the buffer and fails.

maria_g: are we using TextDecoder streaming API in the browser? That helps with multibyte boundaries.

alex_chen: On modern browsers we use ReadableStream.getReader and TextDecoder, but our client assumes a chunk always contains a full event. Need to accumulate until we see a complete JSON object.

samir: can you paste the chunk parser you have?

alex_chen: here's a minimal helper I hacked:

```
let buf = '';
const reader = resp.body.getReader();
const dec = new TextDecoder();
while(true){
  const {done, value} = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  buf += dec.decode(value, {stream: true});
  // naive: split on "\n\n" or event boundaries
  const parts = buf.split('\n\n');
  for(let i=0;i<parts.length-1;i++){
    try{ handle(JSON.parse(parts[i])); }
    catch(e){ console.error('parse fail', e, parts[i].slice(0,80)); }
  }
  buf = parts[parts.length-1];
}
if(buf.trim()) { try{ handle(JSON.parse(buf)); } catch(e){ /* final partial */ } }
```

maria_g: that is similar to what we had in node. Two problems: 1) we need a more robust end-of-object detector (bracket counting) for when events include pretty JSON with newlines, 2) backpressure: the browser UI can fall behind if we parse every tiny chunk.

samir: about bracket counting - we did that in rust client. Maintain a depth counter: increment on '{' '[' and decrement on '}' ']'. When depth==0 and not inside string -> emit. Also need to handle escape sequences.

jordan: dev-bot notice: a deploy to staging happened at 2026-02-10T15:12:01Z which might affect streaming behavior.

alex_chen: thanks. TL;DR: we should (A) make the browser parser resilient to split JSON via depth-based reassembly, (B) ensure reconnect/backoff keeps partial buffer and replays leftover if server supports resume or fallback to re-request with range, (C) add token-refresh race handling so we don't drop in-flight events.

maria_g: for (B) we don't have resume tokens in the protocol. Simpler: when reconnecting due to 401, hold the partial buffer for a short backoff and prefer exponential backoff instead of immediate reconnect. That gives server time to finish.

samir: I can submit a PR to devex-sdk-js with: - bracket-aware streaming parser - optional maxBufferBytes to avoid OOM - a backpressure hook (pause callbacks when queue > N)

alex_chen: awesome. can you add a small test harness? I used ncat to simulate split chunks: `printf 'data: {"delta": "hello' | pv -qL 5 | ncat -l 8080` then later send the remainder.

samir: nice. I'll add an integration that uses a tiny server to emit partial frames and verifies handler receives complete objects.

jordan: ping @maria_g for code review when PR is up.

maria_g: will review, but remind me to check edgecases: strings with braces, escaped quotes, and unicode multibyte boundaries. Also add CI test for Node environment where chunking differs (Buffer vs Uint8Array).

alex_chen: note: Node's stream provides Buffer chunks which may contain multiple events in one chunk; code should handle both. Also watch out for performance: avoid per-byte operations if possible.

samir: plan for PR: 1) new Parser class in sdk/streaming/parser.js 2) tests in sdk/streaming/__tests__/parser.test.js 3) small example app in examples/streaming/resume-sim.js 4) docs update in README streaming section.

jordan: can we also add an opt-in heuristic: if token TTL < 30s, auto-refresh before starting long streams? reduces race.

alex_chen: yep - that's a good mitigation. Add config: streamRefreshThresholdSeconds.

maria_g: FYI I'll also open a separate issue to audit our server behavior on 401 during streams — ideally server should send a final well-formed event before closing where possible.

samir: PR target by EOD tomorrow. reviewers: @maria_g, @jordan.

alex_chen: thanks team. small follow-ups: - samir drop the test commands here when you open the PR - I'll try to reproduce in Safari (quirks with TextDecoder). :eyes:

dev-bot: Reminder: please tag PR with "streaming-parser" label for tracking.

maria_g: got it. :thumbsup:

samir: added the initial branch: github.com/redwood-inference/devex-sdk-js/pull/274 (WIP). contains basic depth parser + tests. still need to bench.

alex_chen: checking it now. quick note: add a stress test that simulates high-frequency small chunks to validate backpressure hook.

maria_g: thanks. once green I'll merge and backport a minimal fix to the 1.x release branch.

alex_chen: perfect. closing loop here. if anyone hits other edge-cases drop them in this thread.
